INT. WESTSIDE MINI-GOLF - WINDMILL HOLE - SUNSET

Fake turf, a creaking windmill, a sign: "HOLE 9 - PAR 3." Plastic gnomes line the rail.

JAMAL, 16, in a faded band tee, leans on his putter. SKYE, 16, purple streaks in her hair, chews gum. MARCUS, 17, stands too close to her. DOLORES, 60s, the attendant in a visor, perches on a stool with a clipboard. PIM, 12, Skye's little brother, crouches to peer into the windmill.

DOLORES
Last group. Hole nine closes in five.

JAMAL
Skye, you're up.

SKYE
(together)
I know.

^MARCUS
I know.

They glance at each other. Marcus looks away first. Skye lines up the putt.

PIM
Don't hit it too hard! The blade will smack it back!

SKYE
Pim, back off.

He takes two steps back. She putts. The ball rolls, enters the windmill, and clatters out the wrong side.

Marcus laughs. Skye glares. Jamal hands her another ball without a word.

DOLORES
(writing)
That's a six.
